d2d1, wined3d: Fewer commands per Direct2D draw.

Each Direct2D primitive still cost a viewport, an index buffer, a vertex
buffer and two constant buffer commands for wined3d's command stream, and
three buffer maps:

- wined3d emitted a viewport command for every RSSetViewports(), unlike
  scissor rectangles, blend and rasterizer states, which it skips when
  they do not change. Skip identical viewports too.
- Direct2D bound its upload buffer as index and vertex buffer at the
  draw's offsets. Vertex data in the upload buffer now starts at a
  multiple of the stride, and draws bind the buffer at offset 0 and pass
  a start index and a base vertex, so wined3d skips the rebinding.
- The vertex and pixel shader constants of a draw go into the constant
  buffer with one map instead of two.

The d2d1 (17491), d3d11 (562560) and d3d9 visual (210933) conformance
tests give the same results before and after. Affinity's interface
renders identically. Vector editing in Affinity under a nested KWin, on
top of 0059, three rounds each, CPU per presented frame on wined3d's
thread / the UI thread:

                 before          after
  drag         3.9 / 7.1 ms    3.5 / 6.6 ms
  node edit    3.6 / 5.4 ms    3.3 / 5.0 ms
  typing       2.0 / 13.0 ms   1.9 / 12.4 ms
  drag frames  373             395

